    "description": "Apartment living in Iran brings household routines into contact with changing architectural layouts and shared residential space. Research highlights negotiations over privacy and social interaction, while showing that building form alone cannot explain residents' experiences.",
    "tags": ["Apartments", "Housing", "Domestic life", "Privacy", "Urban design"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:05:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:05:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Apartment Living in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How Iranian housing research explains apartment layouts, household privacy, shared space, and the limits of architectural claims about everyday life."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "housing-and-routines",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A changing setting for domestic life",
        "content": "An apartment is more than a smaller version of a detached house: its layout establishes different relationships between household activities, visitors, and the spaces beyond the front door. Iranian housing research has examined this relationship as a question of compatibility between buildings and ways of living. A historical analysis published in 2009 argues that housing forms changed more quickly than some domestic habits. [s1]\n\nThat study distinguishes traditional housing, a transitional period, and contemporary apartment development. Its central concern is the mismatch that can arise when a standardized arrangement offers less flexibility than residents need. The author describes adaptation as a two-way process: people may alter their surroundings where possible, or adjust their routines when the building cannot accommodate them. This is an analytical interpretation of housing change, rather than proof that every household followed the same sequence. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "privacy-and-open-plans",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Privacy does not have one fixed layout",
        "content": "A doctoral study of modern high-rise housing in Tehran investigated how privacy and social interaction were expressed inside apartments. Its repository abstract reports a strong distinction between public and private life alongside a more relaxed attitude toward spaces shared by women and men within the home. Respondents' interest in integrated, open-plan interiors complicates the assumption that cultural continuity requires the preservation of every older spatial division. [s2]\n\nThe useful distinction is between privacy from outsiders and separation within a household. They are not equivalent design goals. An open interior can coexist with a strong wish to control access to domestic life. The Tehran findings therefore suggest negotiation and selective adaptation, rather than a simple replacement of tradition by modernity. They remain findings from a particular study, not a description of every Iranian family or apartment. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "shared-space-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What shared space can tell us",
        "content": "Research published in 2026 on the Azadegan Residential Complex in Rasht, a northern Iranian city, shifts attention to circulation outside individual homes. The authors used Space Syntax, a method for analyzing how spaces connect, to examine routes and visibility across the complex. More connected internal routes and recessed peripheral areas presented different opportunities for movement and potential observation by others. [s3]\n\nThe qualification matters: the study treats its spatial measures as proxies. It does not establish actual crime rates or directly validate residents' feelings of safety. A plan can reveal opportunities for encounters and visibility, but interpreting those opportunities as lived experience requires behavioral or perceptual evidence. [s3]\n\nTogether, these studies suggest useful questions for understanding apartment life: how does a home control access, accommodate changing activities, and connect to communal space? Their answers depend on the building and the people using it. Tehran high-rises and a Rasht residential complex provide contrasting cases, rather than a single national model. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
